    </seriesStmt><sourceDesc><p>Born digital.</p></sourceDesc></fileDesc><revisionDesc><change who="http://syriaca.org/documentation/editors.xml#autogenerated" when="2026-07-28-05:00">CREATED: This bibl record was autogenerated from a Zotero record.</change><change who="https://www.zotero.org/cbss_admin" when="2022-11-18T14:33:27Z">Record created in Zotero.</change></revisionDesc></teiHeader><text><body><biblStruct type="journalArticle"><analytic><author><forename>Bradley John</forename><surname>Marsh</surname></author><title level="a">The Story of Susanna in Syriac: A Preliminary Survey of Diversity</title><idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/cbss/D3RZUTYJ</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/a_comprehensive_bibliography_on_syriac_studies/items/D3RZUTYJ</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/4861694/items/D3RZUTYJ</idno></analytic><monogr><title level="j">Aramaic Studies</title><imprint><date>2019</date></imprint><biblScope unit="vol">17:1</biblScope><biblScope unit="pp">15-38</biblScope></monogr><note type="abstract">This pilot study outlines the diverse and at times interwoven textual traditions of the story of Susanna in the Syriac Bible. There are three different versions of the story aside from the Syrohexapla and the revision of Jacob of Edessa, namely the ‘Peshitta’ version, another here dubbed ‘Sinai Susanna’, and finally the so-called ‘Harklean’ version. This paper seeks to describe the kinds of relationships that exist between these textual traditions as well as to suggest what type of critical edition might best display these connections. Additionally, the Sitz im Leben of the ‘Harklean’ version is briefly discussed, reflecting on whether the version’s unique literary features betray a Jewish or a Christian background.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
  Marsh, Bradley John. “The Story of Susanna in Syriac: A Preliminary Survey of Diversity.” <title level="m">Aramaic Studies</title> 17:1 (2019): 15–38.
